#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:174
msgid ""
"**Safety Comments:** Each `unsafe` block is preceded by a `// SAFETY:` "
"comment explaining why the operation is safe. This is standard practice in "
"Rust to aid auditing."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:177
msgid ""
"**String conversions:** The code demonstrates the conversions required for "
"FFI:"
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:179
msgid "`&str` -> `CString`: To create a null-terminated string for C."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:180
msgid "`CString` -> `*const c_char`: To pass the pointer to C."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:181
msgid "`*const c_char` -> `&CStr`: To wrap the returned C string."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:182
msgid ""
"`&CStr` -> `&[u8]` -> `&OsStr` -> `OsString`: To convert the bytes back to a "
"Rust OS string."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:184
msgid ""
"**RAII (`Drop`):** We implement `Drop` to call `closedir` automatically when "
"the iterator goes out of scope. This ensures we don't leak file descriptors."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:186
msgid ""
"**Iterator Interface:** We wrap the C API in a Rust `Iterator`, providing a "
"safe and idiomatic interface (`next` returns `Option<OsString>`) to the "
"underlying unsafe C functions."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:192
msgid ""
"Explain that `CString` owns the data (like `String`), while `CStr` is a "
"borrowed reference (like `&str`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/unsafe-rust/solution.md:194
msgid ""
"The `OsStrExt` trait is needed on Unix systems to convert bytes directly to "
"`OsStr`."
msgstr ""
